### Justification
Multiple authoritative sources, including the European Commission, explicitly state that claims of a pre-planned pandemic are conspiracy theories with no evidence that any individual or organization created the virus ([source](https://commission.europa.eu/strategy-and-policy/coronavirus-response/fighting-disinformation/identifying-conspiracy-theories_en)). Fact-checkers like PolitiFact have debunked allegations linking Bill Gates to the pandemic’s origins ([source](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/04/17/bill-gates-is-top-target-for-coronavirus-conspiracy-theories-report.html)), and there is no evidence that Gates had prior knowledge of SARS-CoV-2 or that pandemic exercises like Event 201 predicted COVID-19 ([source](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/03/26/fact-check-bill-gates-backed-pandemic-exercise-didnt-predict-covid-19/5081854002/)). Scientific consensus indicates a natural zoonotic origin from an animal reservoir, not artificial creation ([source](https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1515/mammalia-2020-0044/html?lang=en)). No documented meetings, emails, or financial transactions link Gates to pandemic planning ([source](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/03/26/fact-check-bill-gates-backed-pandemic-exercise-didnt-predict-covid-19/5081854002/)).